{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/442497-indomitable-vs-awe","id":442497,"title":"Indomitable vs. Awe.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-15T07:46:46-07:00","id":442497,"title":"Indomitable vs. Awe.","content":"I'm having trouble reconciling this.<br /> <br /> From a mechanical standpoint, Indomitable just doesn't seem to apply to the Majesty power &quot;Awe&quot;, since Awe has no roll, so it is neither Contested nor Resisted. But from a <i>thematic</i> and <i>narrative</i> standpoint, Indomitable <i>should</i> have an effect since Awe is, even if passively, &quot;influencing the character's thoughts and emotions&quot;, and it is most certainly &quot;has bearing on her behavior&quot;.<br /> <br /> I think my preferred solution is to treat it as if it gives the character with Indomitable the chance to invoke &quot;Clash of Will&quot; against the vampire using Awe, adding 2 dice to their roll as per the Merit's description. Vampires can already Lash Out to counter Awe, but this would be a passive defense. If a human with Indomitable were resisting, powerful Kindred are likely to punch right through the effort, while young vampires might be surprised at the mental fortitude presented by the mortal.<br /> <br /> Any thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3855-gebbethine","name":"gebbethine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3855_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T07:53:56-07:00","id":442518,"title":"","content":"A two-dot mortal merit should not provoke clash of wills, as a <i>general</i> rule. I do believe that Indomitable would apply to/against the roll for being &quot;called,&quot; IIRC. Not near books. Iron Will is a nice counter to Awe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T08:37:42-07:00","id":442586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SoulGambit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/442497-indomitable-vs-awe?p=442518#post442518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A two-dot mortal merit should not provoke clash of wills, as a <i>general</i> rule. I do believe that Indomitable would apply to/against the roll for being &quot;called,&quot; IIRC. Not near books. Iron Will is a nice counter to Awe.</div> </div> </div> </div>Iron Will suffers from the same problem as Indomitable I posited above: it requires for the to be a roll. There is no roll for Awe. At best, it can help with the Wits + Composure roll. At best. There is no roll for &quot;calling&quot; someone when you're using Awe, so again, inapplicable.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3855-gebbethine","name":"gebbethine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3855_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T09:07:39-07:00","id":442609,"title":"","content":"Ok, you know how Majesty eliminates penalties for social rolls? I'd say that bit doesn't work so well on someone who's indomitable. You shoot a guy in front of one and try to majesty your way out of it? Nah, you're getting the -2.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T09:21:03-07:00","id":442634,"title":"","content":"Awe allows them to add their Majesty rating to Presence only (not Manipulation) Social rolls and cancels penalties due to their current appearance. You don't automatically jump into the sack with them just because they turned on Awe and told you to do it. They still have to make a Contested roll to seduce you (which incidentally is Manipulation not Presence but I've seen more than one ST use Presence for seduction rolls.) and that's when Indomitable comes into play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":190,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/190-audrey-st-john","user":"Audrey St. John","date":". 03-15-2015, 12:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/190-audrey-st-john","name":"Audrey St. John","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T09:38:12-07:00","id":442643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>gebbethine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/442497-indomitable-vs-awe#post442497\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Any thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div> The primary use of Awe is to give social bonuses to Presence. All it means is that you regard the person better - there is actually nothing forcing your character to do anything. The Majesty user still would need to run through Social Maneuvers to get your Indomitable character to do anything. <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, from a story standpoint, it means that you have a strong will that's not easily swayed. So, when the Social Maneuver is over, opt to not do what the Awe user wants, which is entirely valid. Or do it anyways, because they do make a bit of sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T10:41:54-07:00","id":442816,"title":"","content":"M'kay, good points both. Thanks, guys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3855-gebbethine","name":"gebbethine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3855_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}